t 7^6 \\equiv 1 \\cdot 13 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{17}\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(2^{22} \\bmod 17\\). Again, using Fermat's Little Theorem, we get:\n\n\\[2^{16} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{17}\\]\n\\[2^{22} = 2^{16} \\cdot 2^6 \\equiv 1 \\cdot 64 \\equiv 64 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{17}\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(7^{22} - 2^{22} \\bmod 17\\):\n\n\\[7^{22} - 2^{22} \\equiv 13 - 13 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{17}\\]\n\nTherefore, the value is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).\n\nLet proposition $p$: $\\exists x\\in R$, $x-\\sqrt{2}\\leqslant x$. Then, the negation of proposition $p$ is (    )\nA: $\\neg p$: $\\exists x\\in R$, $x-\\sqrt{2} > x$\nB: $\\neg p$: $\\exists x\\in R$, $x-\\sqrt{2}\\geqslant x$\nC: $\\neg p$: $\\forall x\\in R$, $x-\\sqrt{2} > x$\nD: $\\neg p$: $\\forall x\\in R$, $x-\\sqrt{2} < x$\n\nThe given proposition \\( p \\) is:\n\\[ p: \\exists x \\in \\mathbf{R}, \\; x - \\sqrt{2} \\leqslant x. \\]\n\nTo find the negation of this proposition, we need to negate both the existential quantifier and the inequality. The negation of an existential quantifier \\(\\exists\\) is a universal quantifier \\(\\forall\\), and the negation of the inequality \\(x - \\sqrt{2} \\leqslant x\\) is \\(x - \\sqrt{2} > x\\).\n\nThus, the negation of \\( p \\) is:\n\\[ \\neg p: \\forall x \\in \\mathbf{R}, \\; x - \\sqrt{2} > x. \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct choice is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]\n\nIf it takes 5 minutes to prepare each account, and the manager must prepare 24 accounts a day, how much total time will the manager need for work in the first week? Assume he has 10 work days in a week.\n\nTo determine the total time the manager will need to prepare all the accounts in the first week, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the time required to prepare one account.\n2. Calculate the total time required to prepare all 24 accounts.\n3. Determine the number of work days in the first week.\n4. Calculate the total time for the manager in the first week based on the total time to prepare all the accounts and the 